The original Apache 2.0 headers are preserved in each vendored file, and each
file carries a comment naming its upstream path and commit.

    attnbench/_vendor/ruler/scoring.py
        From scripts/eval/synthetic/constants.py. VERBATIM — no modification.

    attnbench/_vendor/ruler/niah.py
        From scripts/data/synthetic/niah.py. MODIFIED. Restructured from a CLI
        script — which calls argparse.parse_args() at *import* time and reads
        its configuration from a module-level `args` Namespace and global RNG
        state — into plain functions taking explicit parameters and a local
        random.Random(seed). Two upstream options raise NotImplementedError
        rather than silently substituting: haystack_mode="essay" (needs NLTK
        and a separately downloaded essay corpus) and type_needle="words"
        (needs the `wonderwords` package).

        ONE CHANGE TO THE ALGORITHM ITSELF, added to this list 2026-09-23 when
        the vendored files were first diffed against upstream at the commit
        they cite. Needle insertion samples
        `min(len(needles), num_haystack)` positions where upstream samples
        `len(needles)`. Upstream therefore RAISES ("Sample larger than
        population") when there is less filler than there are needles; this
        copy silently inserts fewer needles than it has answers for, which
        yields an unanswerable example rather than an error. Measured at
        num_needle_k=4: unanswerable for 74.8% of seeds at num_haystack=1,
        53.8% at 2, 24.8% at 3, and 0% at 4 and above. No result in this
        repository is affected — every measured band is >= 2048 tokens, i.e.
        hundreds of filler sentences — and `accuracy/ruler.py` now derives its
        minimum filler count from num_needle_k so the sizing search cannot
        enter that range. Previously this file said "The needle/haystack
        algorithm is unchanged"; that was true of everything except this.

    attnbench/_vendor/ruler/variable_tracking.py
        From scripts/data/synthetic/variable_tracking.py. MODIFIED, for the
        same import-time reasons. The few-shot/in-context-example machinery
        (`add_fewshot`) is dropped as an orthogonal CLI convenience.

    Not implemented from upstream: common_words_extraction (needs
    `wonderwords`) and qa (needs a separately downloaded QA dataset).

    TASK PARAMETERS THAT DIFFER FROM RULER'S OWN PRESETS. Vendoring the code
    faithfully is not the same as running the benchmark, and the presets this
    harness's tasks are modeled on live in RULER's scripts/synthetic.yaml,
    which is not vendored here. Diffed against it 2026-09-23:

      niah_single  vs niah_single_1   — type_needle_k is "uuids" here and
                                        "words" upstream (wonderwords). Every
                                        other argument matches.
      niah_multikey vs niah_multikey_3 — num_needle_k is 4 here and 1
                                        upstream. Everything else matches
                                        (type_haystack "needle",
                                        uuids/uuids, num_needle_v 1,
                                        num_needle_q 1).
      vt           vs vt              — matches (num_chains 1, num_hops 4).

    The num_needle_k difference is deliberate — four genuine needles hidden
    among same-shaped decoys, which is a harder retrieval task than the
    preset's single needle — but it was undocumented until 2026-09-23, and
    accuracy/ruler.py described this task as matching its preset "exactly — no
    substitution needed for this one". It changes the task, not just the
    vocabulary, and it is the larger of the two deviations.

    Consequence for comparability, stated because it is a methodological
    difference and not a footnote: this harness uses RULER's task-construction
    algorithm with a dependency-free filler-text haystack in place of real
    prose, numeric/UUID needles rather than word needles, and (for
    niah_multikey) four keys rather than one. Filler text is a measurably
    easier haystack to search and four keys is harder than one, so absolute
    accuracy numbers from this repository are NOT comparable to published
    RULER results — in either direction. Every result row records the haystack
    substitution in its `haystack_mode` column.

    Full detail: attnbench/_vendor/ruler/VENDORED.md

